{"id": "10976", "revid": "2236161", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10976", "title": "Englewood (Colorado)", "text": "Englewood is a city of 35,000 people (2019) in the Denver Area of Colorado. Englewood is a full-service city with its own, independent park, library, and public works systems, but little to draw visitors.\nGet in.\nAs part of the Denver Metro Area, Englewood is served primarily by the Denver points of entry (airport, rail, bus, etc.). Getting to Englewood (proper) requires traveling US 85 (Santa Fe Blvd.), Broadway Road, or, US 285 (Hampden Avenue). Note that many areas to the southeast use Englewood as their city designation due to postal assignments. Englewood (proper) centers around the intersection of US 285 and Broadway Road.\nEnglewood is served by many RTD routes, however the primary routes are Light Rail Lines C and D at Englewood Station and Oxford Station, as well as Route 0."}
{"id": "10977", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10977", "title": "Englewood (Florida)", "text": "Englewood is a city with a bustling art community, authentic beach town atmosphere, and an abundance of aquatic activities on the Gulf of Mexico in Southwest Florida. In 2020, it was home to 21,000 people.\nUnderstand.\nEnglewood is enclosed on three sides by the Gulf of Mexico and the larger cities of Venice and Port Charlotte, which both have more shopping and action, but the beaches aren't as nice. Englewood has the necessities including Wal-Mart, Home Depot, Publix, and Bealls.\nFolklore has it that in the 1920s, a candidate for mayor promised that if elected, he would guarantee to have the US 41 run through town. Venice and Port Charlotte took off instead.\nGet in.\nEnglewood is a 30-minute drive south from Sarasota/Bradenton Regional Airport ('), a 60-minute drive north from Fort Myers Regional Airport ('), and a 75 minute drive south of Tampa International Airport ().\nEnglewood is accessible from I-75 from exit 191 River Rd and to a lesser extent 193 Jacaranda Blvd.\nGet around.\nYou'll need to get a rental car from the airport as public transportation is nearly non-existent, and is impossible to use from town-to-town.\nSee.\nPopular entertainment includes the beach, restaurants, and a small shopping district located primarily along Dearborn Street.\nSleep.\nEnglewood isn't big on hotel chains, but it offers a variety of beach resorts."}
{"id": "10978", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10978", "title": "Englewood (New Jersey)", "text": "Englewood is a city in Bergen County, New Jersey. It borders on Fort Lee and Englewood Cliffs to its east, Tenafly to its north, Teaneck to its west, and Leonia to its south. Some sections of the city are quite built-up with office buildings and businesses, but it is also home to the Flat Rock Brook nature preserve (pictured below).\nGet in.\n&lt;mapframe latitude=\"40.886427\" longitude=\"-73.975196\" zoom=\"5\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" /&gt;\nEnglewood is quite close to Upper Manhattan. Only Fort Lee is between Englewood and the George Washington Bridge.\nBy car.\nTake the George Washington Bridge and follow signs for Route 4 West. There are several exits in Englewood from Route 4, including State Route 93.\nBy bus or jitney.\nJitneys that leave from the parking slots just outside the doors of the lower level of the Port Authority George Washington Bridge Bus Station ply Route 4 through Englewood and beyond. There are also NJ Transit buses that come in from the larger Port Authority Bus Terminal on W. 41 St in Midtown Manhattan."}
{"id": "10979", "revid": "705825", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10979", "title": "English Harbour", "text": "English Harbour is a town in southern Antigua.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nVC Bird International Airport is approximately 15 mi away and is served by flights from the UK, US and elsewhere.\nBy taxi.\nTaxi journeys from St John's cost approx US$20 return.\nDo.\nTickets to the Shirley Heights/Dow's Hill Interpretation Centre are US$15/EC$40 as of 2024. They do not accept payment cards on site, but you can pay by card down at the Nelson's Dockyard site.\nClarence House is only accessible by booked tour during season, on Tuesdays &amp; Thursdays at 9:30AM or 3PM. It is closed to tourists otherwise. You can book and pay online[dead link], $20US or $50EC as of 2024.\nThere is a weekly party at Shirley Heights every Friday at 5pm during season, called \"Rum in the Ruins\". You can book and pay online[dead link], $25US or $65EC as of 2024.\nEat.\nPillars Restaurant is next to Nelson's Dockyard. Boom Restaurant is across the harbour, with a free shuttle boat from Pillars. (takes 1 minute). Boom has a lunch deal including pool access for US$95.\nDrink.\nPillar's Restaurant has a bar. There is also the Galley Bar within Nelson's Dockyard.\nSleep.\nThere are numerous options within the town. Energie Apartments has a gym attached with yoga classes included.\nWithin the Nelson's Dockyard complex, there are two hotels, the Cooper &amp; Lumber Store Hotel, and the Admiral's Inn. Admiral's Inn also has rooms on the other side of the harbour, the Gunpowder Suites. They have a free shuttle boat between the two, which takes 1 minute."}
